PROVENANCE - clean-room origin of the sproto protocol implementation
=====================================================================

This file records how the Synology Drive wire protocol ("sproto") implemented in
`internal/sproto` (and the action layer in `internal/actions`) was produced, and
states its non-derivation from proprietary material. It is the compliance-gate
artifact referenced by docs/DESIGN.md §9 and is reviewed alongside LICENSE.

Method
------
The protocol implementation is an independent, clean-room reimplementation built
solely from observation of the wire (the bytes exchanged over the network) and
from the behaviour of a running server. The implementation derives the framing,
the PStream/PObject encoding, the handshake sequence, and the action set from
that observation only.

Non-derivation
--------------
The following were NOT used and NOT copied into this codebase:

  * any proprietary Synology source code, in whole or in part;
  * any private/internal protocol specification document;
  * verbatim identifiers, constant names, error-code tables, struct layouts, or
    comment text taken from such a specification or from decompiled binaries.

Names, constants, and error codes that appear on the wire are reproduced only to
the minimal extent required for interoperability (a protocol cannot be spoken
without sending the bytes the peer expects); they are facts of the wire format,
not creative expression copied from a source.

Scope and security posture
--------------------------
Consistent with docs/DESIGN.md §1/§9, the client speaks to Synology only over
TLS. The plaintext-HTTP fallback and the obfuscated login encryption that exist
in the official protocol are intentionally NOT implemented; the password is sent
in the clear only inside the TLS channel, and the server certificate is pinned at
the application layer (SPKI, SHA-256). No proprietary cryptographic routine is
reimplemented; MD4 (required by the protocol for content hashing) is implemented
from its public RFC 1320 specification (`internal/md4`).

Default exclusion rules
-----------------------
The default exclusions a new pairing starts with (`internal/config/exclude.go`:
the home view's `Photos` and `Backup`, the file and folder names the NAS and the
operating systems keep for themselves, and temporary-file suffixes) match what
Synology's own Drive Client does, so the two behave alike out of the box. They were
established by observing that client as a user can: its setup wizard and logs, the
plain-text rule files it installs, the strings it carries for those folder names,
and its traffic to a test server through a TLS-terminating proxy. Synology also
documents these rules publicly in its Drive Client help ("Sync Task Rules"). No code
was disassembled, decompiled or copied; the lists are facts about interoperable
behaviour, reproduced to the extent needed to match it.

Review obligation
-----------------
Any change that touches `internal/sproto` or `internal/actions` must preserve the
above: contributions derived from proprietary sources or leaked specifications
are not accepted. This statement, the LICENSE (Apache-2.0), and the NOTICE file
are checked together at review time, and their presence is enforced in CI.
